#!/usr/bin/env python3
# `bash tk-hygiene` used to run this file as shell and fail with garbled syntax
# errors instead of a clear message (measured 2026-09-13 and 2026-09-14). The
# string below is a python/bash polyglot: python sees one ordinary (unused)
# string literal and moves on to the real docstring beneath it; bash sees three
# lines it can execute — a no-op, an `echo` to stderr, and `exit 64` — and never
# reaches the python below that line, which is not valid shell.
#
# The guard is the FIRST statement, so plain Python only assigns `__doc__`
# from a bare string literal that comes first — this one, not the real
# docstring below it, which a sibling bin's `__doc__.splitlines()[0]`
# convention (tk-queue, tk-contract) would then read as garbled guard text.
# The line below assigns `__doc__` explicitly so the real text survives.
""":"
echo "tk-hygiene: run it with python3 or directly, not via bash" >&2
exit 64
":"""
__doc__ = """tk-hygiene — the versioning residue a session leaves behind, cleaned without a menu.

Run with `python3 tk-hygiene ...` or `./tk-hygiene ...`; invoking it through
`bash` exits 64 with a one-line message instead of running (see the polyglot
guard at the top of this file).

Two jobs, both automatic and both idempotent, run at the top of a kickoff so the
session opens on a clean tree instead of ending on a tired questionnaire:

  1. AUDIT the forge. A merged branch should die in its own merge, and that is a
     REPOSITORY setting (`delete_branch_on_merge`), not something a session can
     remember to do — whoever presses the merge button is rarely whoever opened
     the branch. This reports the setting for every repo the roster reaches, so
     the fifth project cannot quietly rot what was switched on by hand in the
     first two.
  2. PRUNE the local side. The forge deleting the remote branch leaves the local
     one behind with its upstream marked `[gone]`. That branch is deleted here
     ONLY when the default branch already has everything it carries — either by
     ancestry, or by CONTENT when a squash or a rebase rewrote its commits; see
     `prunable` and `unmerged_content`.
  3. PRUNE the remote side, for ONE shape of branch: `spec/<m>/T<id>`, the
     per-ticket branch a lane cuts a worktree on. `delete_branch_on_merge`
     deletes the head of the pull request, and that head is the LANE's branch;
     a per-ticket branch is never any PR's head, so nothing on the forge ever
     deletes it. Measured 2026-09-02: four alive on one remote after the lane's
     PR merged, and a sibling repo's were deleted by hand.

WHICH REPOS. Two sources, and no repo name written down in either — this repo is
PUBLIC, and the machine's repos are whatever is on its disk. The first is
`tk-roster` beside this script, imported rather than parsed: the roster already
answers "which projects exist on this machine", and a second answer to that
question is a second thing to keep in step. The second is the directory THIS BIN
is installed in, see `own_tree`: the roster sweeps for queues, the plugin's own
clone has none, and that clone was therefore the one repository the audit never
reached — and the only one whose box was off.

WHAT IS NEVER PRUNED, and why each guard exists:

  - a branch with commits of its own — that is unmerged work, and the whole point
    of the `[gone]` signal is that the REMOTE is gone, which says nothing about
    what the local branch still carries;
  - a branch with no upstream — nothing ever said it was merged, so there is no
    evidence to act on. A local-only branch is work in progress by definition;
  - a branch whose upstream is alive — the ordinary case, and silent here;
  - a branch checked out in this repository's OWN working tree — there is no
    second tree to take it out of, so git's refusal is what the report carries;
  - a branch whose LINKED worktree will not come away cleanly — next paragraph.

A PRUNABLE BRANCH CHECKED OUT IN A LINKED WORKTREE has that worktree removed
first, and is then deleted like any other. A lane's worktree outlives the merge
of its pull request, and the branch under it cannot be pruned for as long as the
directory stands: 41 of them were holding branches on this machine on
2026-09-16, which is what the local step was reporting `kept` about. The removal
is `git worktree remove`, NEVER `--force`: a worktree carrying modified or
untracked files is somebody's unsaved work, git refuses to remove it, and the
branch is kept with that refusal as its reason. The worktree is found through
`git worktree list --porcelain` and matched on `branch refs/heads/<name>`, never
by guessing at a path from the branch's name — and the MAIN working tree, which
is the first record git prints, is never a candidate for removal.

THE TREE THIS RUN IS STANDING IN is never removed, however prunable its branch.
A kickoff fires this bin from whatever directory the session opened in, and
removing that directory leaves every later command of that session failing on a
working directory that no longer exists — measured 2026-09-16: `git worktree
remove` pointed at the cwd succeeds, and the shell after it cannot so much as
run `pwd`. Both the current directory and the tree this bin is INSTALLED in are
held back, and the branch is reported kept with that as its reason.

NOTHING IS DELETED UNDER `--dry-run`, a permanent flag rather than a debugging
one: the same run, with every irreversible act named instead of done. A branch
that would go reads `would-prune` rather than `pruned`, and a remote branch
`would-delete` rather than `deleted`, because these lines are quoted one at a
time into a kickoff's own report — and there, a line reading `pruned` over a
branch still on disk is a lie that no banner above it can reach.

ANCESTRY IS NOT THE WHOLE QUESTION. A squash merge replays the branch as one new
commit and a rebase replays it as several, and after either, no commit of the
branch is an ancestor of the default while every line it wrote is already there.
Measured on 2026-08-29 in one clone: of 15 leftover branches, 11 were ancestors
and 4 were squash-merged, and the ancestry rule alone would have kept those 4
forever. So a branch that fails the ancestry test is asked the CONTENT question
before it is kept — see `unmerged_content`.

The prunable check compares against the DEFAULT branch, not against HEAD, which
is why the delete is `-D` rather than `-d`: `git branch -d` measures merged-ness
against whatever HEAD happens to be, and in a repo whose live tree sits on a
feature branch that is a different, weaker question. The guard here is the
narrower one — `rev-list --count <default>..<branch>` is zero, i.e. the branch is
an ancestor of the default — and it is proved by mutation in the suite. Where the
default branch cannot be resolved at all, NOTHING in that repo is pruned: an
un-anchored comparison is not a safer one.

WHAT THE REMOTE STEP WILL NOT DELETE, beyond the content test every branch here
must pass:

  - anything whose name is not `spec/<m>/T<id>` — the lane's own branch
    included, since that one IS a pull request's head and the forge owns it;
  - a branch the remote has moved since this repo last fetched. The candidates
    come from the remote-tracking refs and the live shas from `git ls-remote`,
    and a difference between the two is work this machine has never seen;
  - and the delete carries `--force-with-lease` pinned to the sha that was
    tested, so a push landing between the listing and the delete refuses it.

The candidates are read from LOCAL refs first, so a repository with none of them
never reaches the network at all — and none of this touches the exit code, which
answers about the forge audit and nothing else.

EXIT CODES, so a caller can act on the result without reading the prose. The
first three are `tk-collisions`' convention, kept deliberately: a finding and a
run that did not happen are different answers, and a caller that cannot tell
them apart reports a broken site file as a checkbox left off.

  0  every repo audited answered true
  1  at least one repo answered false — the checkbox is off somewhere
  2  the run did not happen (a rotten site file, say): NOT a finding
  3  no repo answered false, but at least one could not be audited

`false` outranks `could not audit`: a setting known to be wrong is a fact to act
on, while an unreachable forge is a fact about the network.

WHAT THE REPORT NAMES A REPO BY: its path on this machine, never its `owner/repo`
slug. One of the repos the roster reaches is the private tracker's own clone, and
`docs/agents/issue-tracker.md` asks that its slug stay out of shell history and
session transcripts — a report the kickoff is told to quote lands in exactly
those. The slug is still what `gh` is asked about, in an argv that is never
printed and never reaches a shell.
"""

import argparse
import os
import re
import subprocess
import sys

sys.path.insert(0, os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)))
import tk_site      # noqa: E402  (the path above is what makes it importable)

# `tk-roster` carries no `.py` suffix, so it is loaded by path — the same way the
# suite loads the bins it drives
import importlib.machinery  # noqa: E402
import importlib.util       # noqa: E402


def _load_roster():
    path = os.path.join(os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)), "tk-roster")
    loader = importlib.machinery.SourceFileLoader("tk_roster", path)
    spec = importlib.util.spec_from_loader("tk_roster", loader)
    mod = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
    loader.exec_module(mod)
    return mod


tk_roster = _load_roster()

EXIT_OK = 0
EXIT_FINDING = 1
EXIT_FAILED = 2
EXIT_UNAUDITED = 3

GREEN, RED, UNKNOWN = "true", "false", "unknown"
# a FOURTH state, and deliberately not one of the three above: a repository with
# no GitHub remote is not a repository this audit failed to reach, it is one the
# audit has nothing to say about. Folding it into UNKNOWN would put a permanent
# exit 2 on any machine holding one local-only repo, and an exit code that is
# always the same is an exit code nobody reads
NO_FORGE = "no-github-remote"

# every spelling of a GitHub remote that `git remote add` accepts: the https URL,
# the scp-like ssh form (`git@host:owner/repo`), and the ssh:// URL
REMOTE_RE = re.compile(
    r"""^(?:https?://|ssh://)?           # scheme, optional for the scp-like form
         (?:[^@/]+@)?                    # user@, as in git@github.com
         github\.com                     # this bin audits GitHub and says so
         [:/]                            # ':' in the scp-like form, '/' in a URL
         (?P<owner>[^/]+)/               # owner
         (?P<repo>[^/]+?)                # repo, non-greedy so '.git' is not eaten
         (?:\.git)?/?$""",
    re.VERBOSE,
)


# the per-ticket branch of a lane, and deliberately NOT the lane's own
# `spec/<m>-<slug>`: that one is a pull request's head, which the forge deletes
# on the merge. `<m>` is a milestone name and `<id>` a queue item's number
PER_TICKET_RE = re.compile(r"^spec/[^/]+/T[0-9]+$")


def fail(msg):
    """Stop with code 2. Distinct from a finding: the run did not happen."""
    print(f"tk-hygiene: {msg}", file=sys.stderr)
    sys.exit(EXIT_FAILED)


def first_line(err, code, what):
    """The one line worth printing when a subprocess failed.

    git and gh both write multi-line advice to stderr, and a report that pastes
    all of it buries the branch it was talking about."""
    return (err or f"{what} exited {code}").splitlines()[0]


def git(repo, *args):
    """`git -C repo ...` → (returncode, stdout stripped, stderr stripped).

    A git that cannot be run at all is not a repository answering no: it is the
    run not happening, so it stops here rather than being read as a clean tree."""
    try:
        p = subprocess.run(("git", "-C", repo, *args),
                           capture_output=True, text=True)
    except OSError as e:
        fail(f"git could not be run: {e}")
    return p.returncode, p.stdout.strip(), p.stderr.strip()


def repos_of(paths):
    """The git repositories the roster's project paths land in, de-duplicated.

    The key is the COMMON git directory, not the working tree: a worktree is a
    second working tree over ONE set of branches, so keying on the tree would
    walk the same branch list twice and report the second pass as a repo of its
    own. The path kept is the first the roster named, so the report reads in the
    roster's order.
    """
    seen, found = set(), []
    for path in paths:
        code, key, _ = git(path, "rev-parse", "--path-format=absolute",
                           "--git-common-dir")
        if code != 0 or not key:
            continue                      # not a repository: nothing to do here
        if key in seen:
            continue
        seen.add(key)
        code, top, _ = git(path, "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel")
        found.append(top if code == 0 and top else path)
    return found


def own_tree():
    """This bin's own directory — the SECOND source of repositories to audit.

    The roster answers "which project has a QUEUE", and the plugin's own clone
    has none: no session's auto-memory is keyed to it. So it was the one clone
    the audit never reached, which is how it stayed the only repository on the
    machine with `delete_branch_on_merge` off — proved on 2026-08-27, when a
    merge left its branch alive on the remote and someone deleted it by hand.

    Derived from `__file__`, never written down. A list of repository names here
    would be both a leak out of a public repo and a second list to keep in step,
    which is the objection the module docstring raises against any such list.

    LIMIT, taken deliberately: the site file's lists name PROJECTS, and a clone
    with no queue is not one, so `fleet-deny` cannot hold this source back.
    """
    return os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__))


def slug_of(repo):
    """`owner/repo` for the `origin` remote, or None when there is no GitHub one."""
    code, url, _ = git(repo, "config", "--get", "remote.origin.url")
    if code != 0 or not url:
        return None
    m = REMOTE_RE.match(url)
    return f"{m.group('owner')}/{m.group('repo')}" if m else None


def forge_setting(slug):
    """(value, detail) for `delete_branch_on_merge` — value is one of the three
    literals, and `detail` says why when it is UNKNOWN.

    `gh` is resolved through PATH on purpose: the suite puts its own on the front
    of PATH, which is what keeps the whole suite off the network.
    """
    try:
        p = subprocess.run(("gh", "api", f"repos/{slug}",
                            "--jq", ".delete_branch_on_merge"),
                           capture_output=True, text=True)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return UNKNOWN, "no `gh` on PATH"
    except OSError as e:
        return UNKNOWN, f"gh could not be run: {e.strerror}"
    answer = p.stdout.strip()
    if p.returncode != 0:
        detail = first_line(p.stderr.strip() or p.stdout.strip(), p.returncode, "gh")
        return UNKNOWN, detail
    if answer in (GREEN, RED):
        return answer, ""
    return UNKNOWN, f"gh answered {answer!r}, which is neither true nor false"


def default_branch(repo):
    """The ref every prune candidate is measured against, or None.

    Preferred is the remote's own answer (`refs/remotes/origin/HEAD`), because
    that is what the forge merges into. The local branch is the fallback and a
    WEAKER one — a local default sitting behind the remote makes a merged branch
    look unmerged, which keeps a branch that could have gone. That is the safe
    direction, so it is taken silently.
    """
    code, ref, _ = git(repo, "symbolic-ref", "--short", "refs/remotes/origin/HEAD")
    if code == 0 and ref:
        return ref
    for candidate in ("refs/remotes/origin/main", "refs/remotes/origin/master",
                      "refs/heads/main", "refs/heads/master"):
        if git(repo, "rev-parse", "--verify", "--quiet", candidate)[0] == 0:
            return candidate
    return None


def branches(repo):
    """(name, upstream, track) for every local branch, in git's own order."""
    code, out, err = git(repo, "for-each-ref", "refs/heads",
                         "--format=%(refname:short)%09%(upstream:short)%09%(upstream:track)")
    if code != 0:
        return None, err or f"git for-each-ref exited {code}"
    rows = []
    for line in out.splitlines():
        name, _, rest = line.partition("\t")
        upstream, _, track = rest.partition("\t")
        rows.append((name, upstream, track))
    return rows, None


def prunable(repo, name, upstream, track, default):
    """None when the branch may go, otherwise the reason it stays.

    Every guard the module docstring promises is HERE, in one place, so that a
    reader checking whether their branch is safe reads one function — and so a
    mutation of any single guard has one line to be put back in.
    """
    # ONE guard for both "never had an upstream" and "its upstream is alive",
    # deliberately. Written as two, the second covered the first — a branch with
    # no upstream has an empty `track`, which is not "[gone]" either — so no
    # single mutation could delete a no-upstream branch, and the rule the spec
    # cares about ("a branch without an upstream is NEVER pruned") could not be
    # falsified by the harness. A guard that cannot fail is not a proved guard.
    if track != "[gone]":
        return ("no upstream: nothing ever said it was merged" if not upstream
                else f"upstream {upstream} is still there")
    code, count, err = git(repo, "rev-list", "--count", f"{default}..{name}")
    if code != 0:
        return f"cannot be counted against {default}: {err}"
    if count != "0":
        held = unmerged_content(repo, name, default)
        if held is not None:
            return f"{count} commit(s) outside {default}: {held}"
    return None


def unmerged_content(repo, rev, default):
    """None when merging `rev` into `default` would change nothing there,
    otherwise the reason the branch stays.

    `rev` is a branch name from the local step and a SHA from the remote one, so
    it is named for what it is rather than for either caller.

    Asked only of a branch that FAILED the ancestry test, and it is the strongest
    reading of "already merged": merge the branch into the default in memory, and
    compare the result with the default's own tree. Equal means the default
    already carries every line the branch wrote, however its commits were
    rewritten on the way in.

    Comparing the two TIPS instead — an empty `git diff default branch` — answers
    the same only while the default has not moved on since the merge, which it
    always has. That comparison finds the residue of a rebase on a quiet repo and
    misses every squash on a busy one.

    A merge that CONFLICTS is a branch kept: a merge that did not happen says
    nothing about where the branch's work is. Same for a git that cannot answer
    at all — the silent direction of every guard here is to keep.

    The two are KEPT alike and REPORTED apart. `merge-tree` exits 1 for a
    conflict and for a merge it never got to attempt — an unknown flag on a git
    older than 2.38, a ref that vanished — and `tk-collisions` refuses that same
    blur beside its own call to it. Only a run that NAMED a conflict is reported
    as one; the other says the merge was not measured, which is what a reader
    needs to go look at their git.
    """
    code, out, err = git(repo, "merge-tree", "--write-tree", default, rev)
    if code != 0:
        conflict = next((line for line in out.splitlines()
                         if line.startswith("CONFLICT (")), None)
        if conflict:
            return f"does not merge into {default}: {conflict}"
        return (f"the merge into {default} was not measured: "
                + first_line(err or out, code, "git merge-tree"))
    code, tree, _ = git(repo, "rev-parse", f"{default}^{{tree}}")
    if code != 0 or not out:
        return f"the merge into {default} could not be read"
    if out.splitlines()[0] != tree:
        return f"content {default} does not have"
    return None


def candidate(name, upstream, track, default):
    """Whether a branch is worth a line in the report at all.

    A branch whose upstream is alive is the ordinary case and stays silent; an
    upstream that is `[gone]`, or a branch that never had one, is the residue
    this bin exists to name.

    The DEFAULT branch is never residue, however it looks. A repo that was never
    pushed anywhere has a `main` with no upstream, and reporting that as a branch
    held back by a guard describes an ordinary repository as a mess — the report
    is read every kickoff, so a line that is always there teaches the reader to
    skip the block.
    """
    if default is not None and default.rsplit("/", 1)[-1] == name:
        return False
    return track == "[gone]" or not upstream


def remote_candidates(repo):
    """[(branch, sha)] — the per-ticket branches this repo last saw on `origin`.

    Read from the LOCAL remote-tracking refs, which is what keeps an ordinary
    repository off the network: no candidate here and the remote is never asked
    anything. A branch pushed by another machine and never fetched here is
    invisible, and that is the safe direction — this step misses residue rather
    than acting on a ref it has never seen.
    """
    code, out, _ = git(repo, "for-each-ref", "refs/remotes/origin",
                       "--format=%(refname:short)%09%(objectname)")
    if code != 0:
        return []
    found = []
    for line in out.splitlines():
        short, _, sha = line.partition("\t")
        name = short.split("/", 1)[1] if short.startswith("origin/") else ""
        if PER_TICKET_RE.match(name):
            found.append((name, sha))
    return found


def remote_heads(repo):
    """({branch: sha}, None) for what `origin` holds RIGHT NOW, or (None, why).

    The tracking refs say what the last fetch saw; this says what is there. The
    two are compared before anything is deleted, because the whole act of this
    step is irreversible on a machine that is not this one.
    """
    code, out, err = git(repo, "ls-remote", "--heads", "origin")
    if code != 0:
        return None, first_line(err, code, "git ls-remote")
    heads = {}
    for line in out.splitlines():
        sha, _, ref = line.partition("\t")
        if ref.startswith("refs/heads/"):
            heads[ref[len("refs/heads/"):]] = sha
    return heads, None


def prune_remote(repo, dry_run=False):
    """[(action, branch, detail)] — what this repo's per-ticket branches did.

    Empty when there is no candidate, which is the ordinary case and the one
    that must cost nothing: the report block is printed only when this is not.
    """
    candidates = remote_candidates(repo)
    if not candidates:
        return []
    default = default_branch(repo)
    if default is None:
        return [("kept", name, "no default branch to measure against")
                for name, _ in candidates]
    heads, err = remote_heads(repo)
    if heads is None:
        return [("kept", name, f"the remote could not be listed: {err}")
                for name, _ in candidates]
    out = []
    for name, sha in candidates:
        live = heads.get(name)
        if live is None:
            continue          # already gone; only the tracking ref is stale
        if live != sha:
            out.append(("kept", name, "the remote moved it since the last fetch"))
            continue
        held = unmerged_content(repo, sha, default)
        if held is not None:
            out.append(("kept", name, held))
            continue
        if dry_run:
            out.append(("would-delete", name,
                        f"already in {default}, and no PR's head"))
            continue
        code, _, err = git(repo, "push", f"--force-with-lease=refs/heads/{name}:{sha}",
                           "origin", "--delete", name)
        if code != 0:
            out.append(("kept", name, first_line(err, code, "git push --delete")))
        else:
            out.append(("deleted", name, f"already in {default}, and no PR's head"))
    return out


def audit(named):
    """[(path, slug, value, detail)] for every repo, GitHub-remote or not."""
    answers, cache = [], {}
    for path, slug in named:
        if slug is None:
            answers.append((path, None, NO_FORGE, "no GitHub remote named `origin`"))
            continue
        if slug not in cache:
            cache[slug] = forge_setting(slug)
        value, detail = cache[slug]
        answers.append((path, slug, value, detail))
    return answers


def under(child, parent):
    """Whether `child` is `parent` or sits inside it, both resolved of symlinks.

    String comparison alone answers yes to `/a/bc` under `/a/b`, and this decides
    whether a directory is about to be deleted out from under the running
    session, so the separator is part of the question.
    """
    try:
        child, parent = os.path.realpath(child), os.path.realpath(parent)
    except OSError:
        return False
    return child == parent or child.startswith(parent + os.sep)


def standing_on():
    """The directories this run would pull out from under itself by deleting.

    Two, and they are rarely the same one: the directory the session fired this
    bin FROM — a kickoff runs wherever the session opened, which on this machine
    is routinely a lane's worktree — and the tree this bin is INSTALLED in, which
    is a clone that may itself be a linked worktree.

    A cwd that has already been deleted raises rather than answering, and it is
    then no longer a directory anything here could delete.
    """
    grounds = [own_tree()]
    try:
        grounds.append(os.getcwd())
    except OSError:
        pass
    return grounds


def worktrees(repo):
    """([record], None) for `git worktree list --porcelain`, or (None, why).

    A record is the porcelain's own keys — `worktree`, `HEAD`, `branch`,
    `detached`, `locked`, `prunable` — for one tree, and the list is in git's
    order, which puts the MAIN working tree first and every linked one after it.
    That order is what tells the two apart here; a linked tree is not otherwise
    distinguishable from the main one by its path.
    """
    code, out, err = git(repo, "worktree", "list", "--porcelain")
    if code != 0:
        return None, first_line(err, code, "git worktree list")
    found, current = [], {}
    for line in out.splitlines():
        if not line.strip():
            if current:
                found.append(current)
            current = {}
            continue
        key, _, value = line.partition(" ")
        current[key] = value
    if current:
        found.append(current)
    return found, None


def holding_worktree(repo, name):
    """(path, None) for the LINKED worktree `name` is checked out in.

    (None, None) when no linked worktree holds it — the branch is free, and the
    delete may go ahead. (None, reason) when one holds it and must not be
    removed, which is a branch KEPT with that reason in the report.

    The match is on the porcelain's `branch refs/heads/<name>`, never on a path
    built from the branch's name: this answer is what a `rm -rf` of a directory
    is pointed at, and a name that merely looks like a worktree's is not the same
    fact as git saying that tree has that branch checked out.
    """
    rows, err = worktrees(repo)
    if rows is None:
        return None, f"its worktrees could not be listed: {err}"
    ref = f"refs/heads/{name}"
    # `rows[1:]` — the first record is the main working tree, and there is no
    # second tree to take the branch out of. Left in, this would point
    # `git worktree remove` at the repository itself, which git refuses (rc 128,
    # "is a main working tree"); the report that matters there is git's own
    # refusal of `branch -D`, which names the tree holding the branch
    for record in rows[1:]:
        if record.get("branch") != ref:
            continue
        # the porcelain always names a path, and a record without one is left
        # to `git branch -D` to refuse rather than reasoned about here: there is
        # nothing safe to point a removal at
        path = record.get("worktree", "")
        if not path:
            continue
        if any(under(ground, path) for ground in standing_on()):
            return None, f"checked out in {path}, which this run is standing in"
        return path, None
    return None, None


def prune(repo, dry_run=False):
    """[(action, branch, detail)] — what this repo's branches did, and why."""
    rows, err = branches(repo)
    if rows is None:
        return [("kept", "-", f"branches could not be listed: {err}")]
    default = default_branch(repo)
    if default is None:
        return [("kept", name, "no default branch to measure against")
                for name, u, t in rows if candidate(name, u, t, default)]
    out = []
    for name, upstream, track in rows:
        if not candidate(name, upstream, track, default):
            continue
        reason = prunable(repo, name, upstream, track, default)
        if reason is not None:
            out.append(("kept", name, reason))
            continue
        tree, held = holding_worktree(repo, name)
        if held is not None:
            out.append(("kept", name, held))
            continue
        why = f"merged into {default}, remote gone"
        if dry_run:
            out.append(("would-prune", name, why + (
                f"; the worktree {tree} would be removed first, unforced"
                if tree else "")))
            continue
        if tree is not None:
            code, _, err = git(repo, "worktree", "remove", tree)
            if code != 0:
                out.append(("kept", name, f"its worktree {tree} would not come "
                            "away: " + first_line(err, code,
                                                  "git worktree remove")))
                continue
            why += f"; its worktree {tree} was removed first"
        code, _, err = git(repo, "branch", "-D", name)
        if code != 0:
            out.append(("kept", name, first_line(err, code, "git branch -D")))
        else:
            out.append(("pruned", name, why))
    return out


def report_forge(answers):
    print("## forge — delete_branch_on_merge\n")
    if not answers:
        print("no repository on this machine's roster")
        return
    def label(value):
        return value if value == NO_FORGE else f"delete_branch_on_merge={value}"

    width = max(len(label(v)) for _, _, v, _ in answers)
    for path, _slug, value, detail in answers:
        # the PATH, never the slug — see "what the report names a repo by" above
        line = label(value).ljust(width) + f"  {path}"
        if detail:
            line += f"  ({detail})"
        print(line)


def action_width(blocks):
    """How far to pad the action column, from the actions actually present.

    Derived rather than written down: `would-prune` is half again as long as
    `pruned`, and a constant beside a growing set of verbs is the next line to
    go stale.
    """
    return max((len(action) for _, rows in blocks for action, _, _ in rows),
               default=0)


def report_branches(pruned):
    print("\n## local branches")
    if not pruned:
        print("\nno branch with a gone upstream and none without one — nothing to do")
        return
    width = action_width(pruned)
    for repo, rows in pruned:
        print(f"\n{repo}")
        for action, name, detail in rows:
            print(f"  {action.ljust(width)}  {name}  ({detail})")


def report_remote(rows):
    """The remote block, printed only when there is something under it.

    A heading that is there every kickoff with nothing beneath it teaches the
    reader to skip the block — the same argument that keeps the default branch
    out of the local one.
    """
    if not rows:
        return
    print("\n## remote branches — the lane's per-ticket ones")
    width = action_width(rows)
    for repo, out in rows:
        print(f"\n{repo}")
        for action, name, detail in out:
            print(f"  {action.ljust(width)}  {name}  ({detail})")


def load_site():
    """The site file's allow/denylist, or None — never fatal on absence."""
    try:
        return tk_site.load()
    except tk_site.SiteError as e:
        fail(str(e))


def main(argv=None):
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog="tk-hygiene",
        description="Audit the forge's delete-branch-on-merge setting across "
                    "this machine's repositories, prune local branches whose "
                    "remote is gone and whose content the default branch "
                    "already has, and prune the lane's per-ticket branches "
                    "left behind on the remote.",
    )
    parser.add_argument(
        "--no-remote", action="store_true",
        help="skip the remote step. It is the only part of this bin that reaches "
             "the network for git, and on a slow or absent one a kickoff would "
             "wait for it")
    parser.add_argument(
        "--dry-run", action="store_true",
        help="report what would happen and change nothing: no worktree removed, "
             "no branch deleted here or on the remote. A branch that would go "
             "reads `would-prune`, a remote one `would-delete`")
    args = parser.parse_args(argv)

    site = load_site()
    if site is None:
        print(f"tk-hygiene: no site file at {tk_site.site_path()}, so no allow/denylist "
              "— every repository on this machine's roster is audited.", file=sys.stderr)
    _, swept, _, _, _ = tk_roster.sweep(site)
    # the roster first, so the report reads in its order, and this bin's own
    # clone after it — de-duplicated by `repos_of` when it is one of them anyway
    repos = repos_of([path for _, path in swept] + [own_tree()])

    answers = audit([(repo, slug_of(repo)) for repo in repos])
    report_forge(answers)

    pruned = []
    for repo in repos:
        rows = prune(repo, dry_run=args.dry_run)
        if rows:
            pruned.append((repo, rows))
    report_branches(pruned)

    remote = []
    if not args.no_remote:
        for repo in repos:
            rows = prune_remote(repo, dry_run=args.dry_run)
            if rows:
                remote.append((repo, rows))
    report_remote(remote)

    # `false` outranks `unknown`, per the exit codes at the top of this file
    values = [value for _, _, value, _ in answers]
    if RED in values:
        return EXIT_FINDING
    if UNKNOWN in values:
        return EXIT_UNAUDITED
    return EXIT_OK


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
